WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:05.520
Post memes, and your content might get flagged.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.240
Stop posting memes, and your account will remain safe (at least for a while!)

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:14.099
But think about posting your memeless videos many years from now and ask yourself:

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:17.759
Would you be willing to trade all those days for just one chance?

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:25.980
One chance to come back here and tell them: that they may copyright strike our accounts, but they will NEVER KILL OUR MEMES!!

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.140
Hello Internet! Welcome to Film Theory.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.620
The show that, quite honestly, might not be able to air in Europe anymore...

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.860
That's not an opening joke or an exaggeration either.

00:00:40.877 --> 00:00:43.650
Do you watch gamers or film criticism channels?

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:47.440
Have you ever laughed at a meme: Spongebob, Thanos, or other ones?

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:49.260
Do you have a Twitter or Tumblr account?

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.336
Have you ever used anything from Imgur, Jiphy?

00:00:51.336 --> 00:00:53.459
(or Giff-y depending on how you pronounce it)   (It's Jiphy BTW)

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.103
Are you one of those people who long for the old days of YouTube?

00:00:56.103 --> 00:00:58.600
When the big media companies weren't on the platform?

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:03.560
I am sure, between all of those questions, I am talking to a *huge* chunk of you.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:07.160
And if I am, then pay *very* close attention to today's episode

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:12.500
Because a huge percent of the content that you currently watch and share online is on the chopping block

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:16.500
Including this show, and Game Theory, and GT Live,

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.739
And if there's any hope of saving them, well, we've gotta act now.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.960
So if this is such a big deal,

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.720
Then why is no one talking about this supposed looming threat to all our online content?

00:01:26.729 --> 00:01:31.660
Well, I mean, the name alone is "The Directive on Copyright in the Digital Single Market"

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:33.540
Which, hoo-boy!

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:34.580
If that doesn't get you hyped.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.700
Well, then you don't even policy-make bro.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.080
In short, it's kinda complicated.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.160
By "kinda complicated" I mean really complicated

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:42.040
But you know me!

00:01:42.043 --> 00:01:47.140
I eat complicated topics for breakfast and then spit 'em back at you in a simplified more digestible way

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:50.548
Just like a mama bird puking up little worm nuggets of knowledge!

00:01:50.548 --> 00:01:54.280
So let's get down to it, before this show goes the way of Spider-Man on Titan!

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:58.420
Cue the Thanos snap transition (since it might be one of the last times we can use it!)

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.640
I'm-I'm not feeling so good, Internet!

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:08.899
You may have heard the words "Article 13" tossed around on and off throughout this year.

00:02:08.899 --> 00:02:09.890
If you follow me on Twitter,

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:14.320
You may have seen me trying to sound the alarms about this supposed "Meme Ban"

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.729
A piece of legislation that, according to Facebook,

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:22.028
"Could have serious unintended consequences for an open &amp; creative internet"

00:02:22.028 --> 00:02:23.899
and according to YouTube's CEO,

00:02:24.239 --> 00:02:30.319
"Poses a threat to creators' livelihoods and all of our ability to upload videos to share our voices with the world."

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:34.000
But then, what is this thing that everyone is so worried about?

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:35.260
Well, let me explain.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:39.180
Right now, all websites, and I mean ALL websites including big ones,

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:43.220
like YouTube, Tumblr, Facebook, Instagram, Snapchat, Reddit, and Twitter.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.860
They all rely on two key legal principles to operate:

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:48.800
"Fair Use" and "Safe Harbor".

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.140
Fair Use is one that probably all of you watching are familiar with at this point,

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.300
since everyone on YouTube claims that their video is protected by it.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:59.060
"Fair use is very, very easy to understand."

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:02.300
"I'm Alex from I Hate Everything, and where's the fair use?"

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.849
"So we will be the first client of this account..."

00:03:04.849 --> 00:03:09.180
"...That will be known as the Fair Use Protection Account also known as FUPA."

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.680
But what is it exactly?

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:16.280
Well, it allows for the use of copyrighted materials, without the permission of the original copyright holder.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:19.240
You may have seen some video creators specifically go out of their way

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.040
to state that their use of clips is for educational purposes

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:23.308
"Now with all you've just learned..."

00:03:23.308 --> 00:03:26.540
"(in this video that I have made for educational purposes)"

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.269
And that's because educational use

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:32.400
along with criticism and review, commentary, news reporting, and research

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.520
are all things that are explicitly allowed by United States Fair Use Doctrine.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:38.980
It's how I'm able to show "Gravity Falls" clips like this one,

00:03:38.992 --> 00:03:40.740
and cut-outs of characters like this one,

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.960
when crafting theories about how the show isn't over,

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.360
because the show isn't over.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:45.300
It shouldn't be over.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:46.615
Please don't tell me it's over,

00:03:46.615 --> 00:03:48.100
I refuse to believe that it's over!

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.720
Please bring back "Gravity Falls"!

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.220
or how "Cinemasins" can show clips from a movie to sin them,

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:54.520
or how "Honest Trailers" can show clips from a movie to....

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:56.119
...honestly ...trailer  ...them.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:04:01.260
It's how we're able to edit together "Spongebob Squarepants" with the "Super Smash Brothers - World of Light" trailer

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.440
to have Squidward suddenly getting vaporized mid-bath.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:08.380
or how Thanos can snap his fingers, to dust away "The Emoji Movie".

00:04:08.519 --> 00:04:10.160
Thank you for that, by the way.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:11.929
But, what if something doesn't fall under fair use?

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:14.840
Like if I just straight up re-upload a chunk of "Harry Potter"?

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.620
Well, that's where safe harbor comes in.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:21.620
You see, according to the United States Digital Millennium Copyright Act or DMCA,

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:27.640
Media companies can issue takedown notices to website owners, requesting the removal of infringing content on their site.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.660
When someone uploads a 24 hour livestream of "Family Guy" to YouTube,

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:35.000
YouTube isn't actually responsible for committing that copyright violation.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:38.220
They're only responsible for removing it.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:40.640
That is the principle of "Safe Harbor"

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.160
That YouTube here is just an intermediary.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:47.380
It's not at fault for someone misusing the system and uploading illegal stuff,

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:51.680
as long as it gets taken down and, as such, they're shielded from copyright liability.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:55.880
That's why creators get copyright strikes, and not YouTube.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.360
And just know that when I say "YouTube" I really also mean

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.700
SoundCloud, Imgur, Facebook, Twitter, Tumblr, Reddit,

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.490
pretty much any site that allows users to upload content.

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:06.949
Now, there's one problem that you might have noticed with what I just described.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:11.340
*That all of these laws are in the United States.*

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:15.180
(The United States and Fair Use Doctrine, the United States Digital Millennium Copyright Act)

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.040
Europe has some very different policies.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:21.940
Policies that the Article 13 "Meme Ban" threatens to make even stricter.

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:26.260
You see in Europe, it's not called "Fair Use", it's called "Permitted Use".

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:29.620
Now, that at first might seem like an insignificant word change,

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:35.400
but just the name "Permitted Use" actually speaks to how the European doctrine differs from the U.S.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:40.340
Think about what the word "Permit" means.  It means that you need *permission* to do something

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:44.400
For example, you're not allowed to park somewhere without a *permit* from the city.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.020
Home improvements require a construction *permit* from the government.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:52.780
Basically, "You're not allowed to do this unless we say that you're allowed to",

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.120
and the same applies to copyrighted works.

00:05:55.130 --> 00:06:00.499
You're not allowed to use them, except we're explicitly and expressly permitted to do so.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:04.260
And I think you can start to see where the system might fall apart.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:06.980
So, where are you permitted to use this stuff?

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:12.340
Well, as stated before, the US Fair Use Doctrine specifies that for purposes

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:15.894
such as criticism, comment, news reporting, teaching, scholarship, or research,

00:06:15.894 --> 00:06:17.920
the use of a copyrighted work is considered "fair".

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:21.170
However, an important fact here is that the list isn't exclusive.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:25.860
In other words, the U.S. Fair Use Doctrine is written in a way that basically says

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:30.160
"Lots of things can qualify as 'Fair Use', here are some examples."

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:34.300
This in turn, allows the law to remain flexible and include new forms of media

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:37.320
that have arisen since it was written, like memes or vlogs

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:41.320
Which didn't exist back when U.S. copyright law was first being drafted.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:46.240
By contrast, the European Permitted Use Policy takes the completely opposite approach.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:50.860
According to the EU's "Copyright in the Information Society Directive of 2001"

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:55.960
Use for parody, quotation, private copying or classroom use are permitted.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.480
At first, that seems pretty similar to U.S. Doctrine,

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:02.860
but then you realize, that's it. That is the whole list.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:07.120
Anything that is not on that list is automatically not allowed.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.820
Under US law, you could take something like this scene...

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:11.220
"What the hell are you?"

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:14.840
"We are Venom."

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:17.140
And remix it to do something like this.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:19.000
"What the hell are you?"

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:21.560
"It's-a me, Mario!"

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:23.340
Quality meme! 10 out of 10!

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:24.340
clap clap!

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:25.680
and U.S. lawyers would say

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:28.910
"Well, this might technically not be a criticism or commentary,"

00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:32.899
"But it's not like this person is trying to take anyone else's work and pass it off as their own..."

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:34.520
"Fair Use!"

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:37.300
European Union doctrine, as it's written, says

00:07:37.301 --> 00:07:38.600
"Is it for a classroom?"

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:39.919
"Is it a quotation?"

00:07:39.919 --> 00:07:41.260
"Is it parody?"

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.800
Well then no permit for you, haaa!"

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:46.139
Oh, and that last one about parody might be a bit confusing

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:48.160
But the legal definition of parody is actually very strict.

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:52.380
The best example is something like a parody song. In order to officially qualify,

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.800
the new parody version of the song needs to have lyrics that directly make fun of the original work.

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.940
So it's a very limited, narrow, definition.

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:02.269
In short, Europe's copyright doctrine just tends to be a lot stricter.

00:08:02.269 --> 00:08:06.840
But Article 13 is what threatens to make a bad problem even worse,

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:10.720
because now it's actively targeting the idea of "Safe Harbor".

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:12.350
It directly states that, quote,

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:17.359
"Online content sharing service providers..." (i.e. sites like YouTube, Tumblr, Reddit, whatever,)

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:18.488
"...and rights holders,.."

00:08:18.488 --> 00:08:20.870
(the movie studios, music labels, etc)

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:25.820
"...shall cooperate in good faith, in order to ensure that unauthorized protected works..."

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:29.440
"... or other subject matter are not available on their services."

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:33.733
So basically, all these sites need to work to ensure that copyrighted stuff isn't uploaded.

00:08:33.733 --> 00:08:34.960
That shouldn't be that big of a deal, right?

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:36.600
YouTube has Content ID,

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:40.200
which protects against wrongful uploads, sometimes a bit too aggressively,

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:44.780
but certainly enough to ensure that I can't find full episodes of "Phineas and Ferb" on here without paying.

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:45.754
It should be covered.

00:08:45.754 --> 00:08:50.450
And meanwhile, sites like Dailymotion where you can find literally all sorts of illegal uploads on,

00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:53.059
will be held accountable and need to clean up their act.

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:55.300
It all sounds reasonable, right?

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:56.740
But here's the trouble.

00:08:56.740 --> 00:09:00.229
You see, an earlier version of Article 13 mentioned...

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:03.380
"Proportionate content recognition technologies"

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:06.540
Which sounds like what YouTube already has in place.

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:09.619
Automatic detection of recognized content upon upload.

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:11.200
And if that was actually what was written,

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:12.400
well, then things would be fine.

00:09:12.410 --> 00:09:14.600
I wouldn't be making this episode.

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.360
But this didn't make it into the version that passed.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:19.219
The people behind Article 13,

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:20.680
(Which is mostly the music industry,

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:24.020
because, quite frankly, the music industry tries to kill anything that it finds threatening)

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:28.560
Wants to keep the language vague, because then, they can demand basically anything they want

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.900
And say, "Well, the law requires you to cooperate in all good faith with us!"

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:34.420
What does good faith mean?

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:35.756
It's unclear.

00:09:35.756 --> 00:09:37.940
It's poorly defined, and that's the problem!

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:43.720
What they're demanding is, that nothing that has the potential to be infringing, be uploaded onto these websites.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:47.840
Catching it via Content ID once it's on the system isn't good enough.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:51.260
They want to stop it before it even gets uploaded.

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:53.040
So what does that translate to?

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:57.280
Well, it has the potential to mean the end of all user-generated content.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:09:59.240
The stuff that we all make.

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:01.640
I mean think about it: Who loses in this situation?

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:04.000
Not the movie studios or the big media companies.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:06.260
They're all permitted to use their own rights.

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:09.380
Not the music industry. They just own the songs.

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:11.060
It's us. All of us.

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:12.100
We all lose.

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:17.300
It's the people on YouTube and Tumblr and TikTok and Twitter and whatever, we lose.

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:21.620
In a conversation I personally had with YouTube CEO Susan Wojcicki about Article 13,

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:24.100
She made it very clear.

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:26.380
If this ends up going through with the current legal language,

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:29.599
YouTube will have to treat Europe like a bubble.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:33.050
Videos uploaded outside of Europe won't be able to go in, and in Europe,

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:36.820
the only people who will really be permitted to upload will end up being the big companies.

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:39.220
If you need even more proof, she even wrote about it:

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:44.640
"If implemented as proposed, Article 13 threatens hundreds of thousands of jobs..."

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:47.880
"...European creators, businesses, artists and everyone they employ."

00:10:48.020 --> 00:10:54.720
"The proposal could force platforms like YouTube to allow only content from a small number of large companies."

00:10:54.860 --> 00:11:00.680
"It would be too risky for platforms to host content from smaller original content creators..."

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:04.740
"...because the platforms would now be directly liable for that content."

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:09.640
The safe harbor shield that was protecting YouTube from copyright strikes may dissolve,

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:11.840
and because their butts are on the line for all copyright strikes now,

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:17.060
the only people YouTube can allow to upload, are the people they are certain owns the IP rights:

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:18.300
The big companies.

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:21.080
And all other sites would have to follow suit.

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:24.480
And it is expensive and difficult to make an appropriate Content ID system.

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:29.020
Even worse, it could empower media companies to make more erroneous copyright claims.

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:34.220
Such as the case where Sony Music Entertainment went after people posting classical music composed by Bach.

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:38.360
What's more, we usually think about copyright protection as it applies to movies and music,

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:42.200
but simple images are covered by copyright as well

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:47.120
Every painting, every drawing, every logo that's posted to the Internet is copyrighted material.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:48.200
Every tattoo!

00:11:48.209 --> 00:11:51.940
(That's not an exaggeration, tattoos are actually owned by the artist who made them.)

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:55.828
And of course: each and every meme.

00:11:55.829 --> 00:11:57.000
Now, I know what a lot of you are thinking.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:11:59.069
Boy, that sucks for people over in Britain,

00:11:59.069 --> 00:12:01.180
(Wait, they're not part of the EU. Hold up.)

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:03.680
(Would Britain be affected by Article 13?)

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:05.860
(Yeah, apparently they would be, alright.)

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.680
Sucks for people over in Britain, France and Spain, but why should I care?

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:11.503
Well, you might not be European,

00:12:11.503 --> 00:12:15.860
But the sites you use operate in countries that are part of the EU.

00:12:15.860 --> 00:12:18.160
And when different countries have different laws,

00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:21.360
Companies usually cater to the least common denominator.

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:24.105
Rather than having different policies for users in different countries,

00:12:24.105 --> 00:12:28.060
Companies will just usually target the strictest set of rules and comply with those,

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:29.660
Then apply that set of rules to everybody.

00:12:29.769 --> 00:12:32.309
For a recent example of this, earlier this year

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:35.576
You probably got a flood of emails from Facebook, Google, Reddit

00:12:35.576 --> 00:12:38.579
And pretty much every website under the sun notifying you

00:12:38.579 --> 00:12:41.909
"Hey, we've updated our privacy policy. Click that you accept it!"

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:45.449
Well, the fact that you got all these emails around the same time wasn't a coincidence.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:50.440
All of these companies were updating their privacy policy to comply with GDPR-

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:53.380
the General Data Protection Regulation passed in May

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:56.280
by -you guessed it- the European Union!

00:12:56.280 --> 00:13:02.460
These sorts of privacy and copyright changes affect all users, not just those based in the EU.

00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:06.920
And trust me, once media companies realize that they can take us down in one part of the globe,

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:11.400
I guarantee, they'll be knocking down the door trying to make it happen in more corners of the earth.

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:16.920
So, European or not, the EU Copyright Directive is something that should matter to you.

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:20.660
Threatening your memes, your online shows, and most importantly,

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:25.840
The literally hundreds of thousands of people who make their living working online.

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:27.720
Me. The whole Theorist Team.

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:30.080
Every YouTuber that you watch.

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:31.500
So what can you do at this point?

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:32.860
Well, there are three things.

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:35.460
First and most importantly, spread the word!

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:38.580
